Okay, so now that I've actually had Nethack 3.1.1 for a while, and actually
played it, I guess I can offer some constructive commentary on the game.
(I've also mailed this to the bug-reports people, because there are some
bugs reported, natch, at the end of the file.)  No incredible commentary
here, folks, just one hacker's observations.

SPOILER WARNING FOR NETHACK 3.1.1!

Things I like about the new Nethack...

 * The line-of-sight thing in the town (Gnomish Mines) is neat; if the
   watchmen don't see you busting a door, they won't give you trouble for
   it.  BTW, is it true that drinking from a fountain will get you in
   trouble in town?

 * Dropping stuff on stairs and watching it bounce down to the next level.
   Cute touch, dev team.

 * Monsters are total suckers for traps!  One time, when I was badly hurting
   in the Gnomish mines, I n50. and while I'm waiting, six rothes and a
   gnome walk in and obligingly fall down a trapdoor trap I hadn't spotted
   yet!  I can understand the first one, but the rest shoulda dodged or
   something.  Not that I'm complaining, mind.  (;3

 * The usual Nethack mayhem, natch.  Cool game, Dev folks!

Stuff I thought was unusual, or didn't like:

 * Prayer doesn't seem to be as useful as it used to be.  Several times now,
   I've been blinded or hallucinated, and even with my first prayer, can't
   get bailed out.  In fact, the only ailment I *have* been able to pray
   away is hunger.  What's the deal?  You need to be lucky now as well as
   have a happy god?  Or was it just a new moon?

 * Pets can be paralyzed by floating eyes now too.  Interesting, even if
   it makes offing the things that much harder.  Wouldn't it be possible
   to close your eyes, sort of like a blindfold that doesn't always work?
   "As you swing, you sneak a peek... you are frozen by the floating eye's
    gaze!"

 * I found a -1 ring of gain strength... that wasn't cursed.  Weird, that.
   Kept it around for possible enhancement (weilding it and casting enchant
   weapon does the trick, right?)

 * The town ecology is kind of strange.  You've got these gnomes and orcs
   running around the town trying to kill you, and the watchmen just stand
   there?  Either the gnomes shouldn't go into town, or the watchmen should
   fight them if they spot them fighting you, or they should be on the
   gnome's side, or something.  Not just stand there watching you beat up
   the town.  (Maybe they should beat up *both* sides?)

Things that really bug me:

 * First level.  Certainly not a bones level.  Almost finished mapping the
   level, just one more door to check.  Kick it down...

     The goblin zaps a wand!  --more--
     You die...  --more--

   Aaagh!  What's a frenning death ray wand doing on the first level?  Such
   powerful weaponry simply shouldn't exist on the first level, at least
   not just lying around (I can see getting it with a wish...)  I firmly
   believe that every character should have a chance, no matter how slim,
   of ascending.  This is not giving it.

 * There's a bug involving the - key; it seems to have the effect of
   stepping on your square again.  I noticed it when I drew Elbereth with
   my fingers, went to type n50. to get some health, and hit - instead of
   0.  I got a rapid-fire series of messages showing the writing being
   obliterated as if I'd stepped on it five times.  This could be deadly!
   (Say, if you're standing on a dart trap...)  Fix this, Dev Team!

 * The sleep dichotomy mentioned in countless other messages.  A wand of
   sleep should not be a wand of death in the hands of a goblin; give our
   brave adventurer a chance of waking with each hit inflicted.  Similar
   argument for floating eyes; if you want them to be dangerous, let them
   initiate gaze attacks or something.  None of this harmless-or-fatal smeg.
   (Oh, and you should be able to wake your dog up by, say, kicking it, or
   blowing a whistle or something.)

Stuff that I'd like to see added or improved:

 * How about a surrender option when faced with shopkeepers who've mistaken
   you for a previous hacker, town watchmen who're ticked off about broken
   doors, Keystone cops, et cetera, et cetera?  Either they get a free round
   of pumming on you, or you get a chance to get off, by paying or some
   such?  (In the mistaken identity case, if your luck's good, the shop-
   keeper figures out her mistake, and lets you go...)

 * Oops.  Broke a door in town, and now the watchmen are mad.  Well, gee,
   I'm sorry, fellas.  Look, I just made a killing selling off gnomish
   armor and stuff; why don't I just pay for a new door, okay?  What?
   Breaking a door's a capital offense?  I can't just buy you a new one?
   Oh well... (pound pound grind)  Sorry about that, fella.  Wish you'd
   taken that p option...

 * I want a command to attack things with weapons!  Where is it written
   that I can't chop through that locked door with my +4 battle-axe, smash
   that statue to pieces with Mjollnir, or the like?  I think it's sick
   that someone armed with Vorpal Blade has to resort to *kicking* to try
   to get a door open.  You don't even have to add a new command, just
   expand (a)pply.

 * Blindness is very inconsistent and non-intuitive.  Why is it that you
   can know to swing your +5 Cuisinart at your poor innocent dog, but can't
   tell from what direction that frost giant is pounding you?  I think the
   following behavior changes need to be made in blindness:

    - If you bump into something while wandering around blind, you should
      either be given the option of attacking it, or (my preference) should
      stumble on it, perhaps kicking it.  Combine this with the (a)pplied
      weapon idea earlier for dealing with enemies when you know they're
      there.  (If your pet can't take a few kicks, what good is it doing?)

    - If something attacks you while you're blind (melee only, possibly),
      some sort of symbol should appear where it is, indicating where to
      bash back.  If you ram said indicator, you'll attack as normal,
      instead of the behavior above.  (I'm tired of being hit by a giant,
      then attacking my dog in retribution.  I think I'd notice if I got
      punched from behind as opposed to in front.)

Well, that about does it for now; thanks for reading my ramblings!  Oh,
